WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.580
Imagine you get sent, day in and day out,
to a random company to work without pay.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:11.220
Each workday starts with an hour-long meeting
followed by another meeting, and another meeting

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.020
and so on and so forth.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:19.920
All meetings are on different, seemingly random
corporate objectives: focus on x, improve

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.540
y, remember z.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.320
Your primary job: sit down, shut up, listen
to your supervisor and take note.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.960
And when you think that the day is finally
over, you are told to take some of the work

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:34.460
home.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.220
Sounds like a horrific corporation doesn’t
it?

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:44.400
This, however, is the perceived reality of
many young people in what's called a twenty-first

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.420
century school.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.180
So how did we happen to end up here?

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:59.340
Frederick the Great, the King of Prussia,
came up with the idea of modern factory schools.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:05.760
The emperor was inspired by Plato who argued
that the ideal city requires ideal individuals,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.360
and ideal individuals require an ideal education.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:18.240
And so the King introduced compulsory education
in 1763 with the mission to unify Germany,

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:24.060
increase literacy, and boost the education
of all its citizens - including the very poor.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:31.920
The early Prussian school system provided
eight-years of instruction in reading, writing,

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.840
music and religious studies.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.520
It imposed a strict ethos of duty, discipline
and obedience.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:39.900
And that worked.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:48.240
The model turned into a big cultural, economical
and military success that inspired rulers

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.620
from Tokyo to Washington.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:57.420
Not long after, state officials all around
the world started writing curriculums, and

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.440
public education became both: a child’s
right and duty.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:09.600
In 1938 the Germans then went one step further
and made school attendance compulsory.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.700
Ever since it’s not parents, but the state
that makes sure kids go to school.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:23.760
Today 85% out of all adults worldwide are
literate and access to information is abundant.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.900
Should government officials still have a monopoly
on how we learn?

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:33.180
A growing number of parents don’t think
so and want to take control over their own

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.260
child’s education.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.740
They advocate for what’s called unschooling.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:45.720
Unschooling is the process of learning through
life, outside institutionalized classrooms.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.920
Similar to homeschooling it usually happens
at home or in other places.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:55.200
But while homeschooling usually follows a
formal curriculum, unschooling doesn’t.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:03.480
Unschoolers learn through their natural life
experiences including play, chores, travel,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:10.320
books, elective classes, board-games, internships,
mentors, and living in a community.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.300
Parents or tutors play a big role.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:20.940
They provide resources, facilitate the learning
experiences, and help the child to reflect

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:21.900
on their progress.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.920
So how come parents choose to unschool their
children when it’s much more convenient

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.120
to send them to school?

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.460
There are 6 key reasons.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.560
Schools can negatively affect the parent-child&nbsp;

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.680
relationship, often replacing&nbsp;
it with an atmosphere

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:41.340
of fear.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:49.140
Institutionalizing kids in a one-size-fits
all classroom is an inefficient use of their

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.240
time and potential, because it requires each
one to learn a subject in a particular way,

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.820
and at a pace that doesn’t take into account
a child's interests or any pre-existing knowledge

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:00.600
they might have.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:08.160
In school, children are primarily taught how
to follow instructions, which means they don’t

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:10.080
practice doing things independently.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.460
Unschoolers have to solve problems by themselves
all the time.

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:16.740
They learn to work autonomously.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.080
School children are told all their lives what
to do.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:27.840
As a result they later often have no idea
what they want and hence simply conform.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.040
Unschoolers practice making their own decisions
every day.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:37.020
They learn to know what they want and are
more likely to question social ills.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.860
School focuses almost exclusively on what
to learn all day.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:48.600
Unschoolers learn to ask the much more important
question: why learn it?

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:55.380
Schools cover only a few subjects someone
thought of a few hundred years ago.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:05:01.320
Unschoolers can explore way more interests,
and look into topics at far greater depth.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:09.660
John Holt, a pioneer of unschooling, said
it as follows: "The anxiety children feel

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:15.420
at constantly being tested, their fear of
failure, punishment, and disgrace, severely

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:20.880
reduces their ability both to perceive and
to remember, and drives them away from the

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:25.920
material being studied into strategies for
fooling teachers into thinking they know what

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.000
they really don't know.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:30.300
What do you think?

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.240
What are the good and bad sides of unschooling?

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:38.940
And more broadly, should the state or parents
decide what to teach a child and what to leave

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.460
out.
Who should have the ultimate say?

